{
  "title": "Questions to Ask When Viewing a House: Checklist",
  "metaDescription": "Use this customized, printable house viewing checklist to generate questions to ask when viewing a house, track red flags, and compare properties.",
  "h1": "Questions to Ask When Viewing a House",
  "introText": "When you first view a house, ask the agent why the owners are selling, how long it has been listed, whether offers fell through, the age of major systems, past water or insurance claims, and real annual utility, tax, and HOA costs. At the same time, check with your own eyes for ceiling water stains, damp smells, wall or foundation cracks, sloping floors, sticking doors, low water pressure, electrical panel scorch marks, and exterior drainage away from the foundation. Evaluating every property against the exact same points gives you a reliable way to compare homes before you make an offer.\n\nThe tool on this page lets you create a custom house viewing checklist tailored to the property you plan to tour. Select the home type, the build era, the state, and any local climate risks like freezing winters, hurricane coasts, or wildfire areas. The tool automatically removes irrelevant checks, such as dropping exterior roof checks for condos or lead paint warnings for newer builds, and lets you log answers and issues for up to three houses. When you are ready to attend a showing or open house, tap the Print or save as PDF button to take your customized sheet with you.",
  "toolHeading": "Your House Viewing Checklist",
  "toolSubheading": "Select the home type, build year, and local climate conditions to build your custom walkthrough sheet.",
  "howItWorks": "Our house viewing checklist puts direct questions for the agent or seller first, ordered by our editorial judgment of how costly each is to miss. Asking why the owners are selling and how long the property has sat on the market reveals whether you have negotiation leverage. Inquiring about previous offers, recent price cuts, or deals that fell apart helps uncover inspection problems that broke previous deals. Knowing the exact age of the roof, heating system, air conditioning unit, and water heater tells you which big replacements you might face first. Inquiries about past basement leaks, burst pipes, and insurance claims are vital, as unaddressed water damage remains one of the most expensive surprises after a purchase.\n\nChecking the physical structure yourself verifies whether the real-world condition matches the seller's claims. When you walk through each room, look closely for water stains on ceilings, around window frames, and under sinks, paying extra attention if someone painted over a single ceiling patch to conceal an active leak. Musty, damp, smoke, or pet smells point to moisture, mold, or odors that are hard to remove. Outside and on the main level, watch for wide, stair-step, or horizontal cracks in the foundation, noticeable floor slopes, and sticking doors that are worth an inspector's opinion. You should also test the shower and sink taps for water pressure, inspect the electrical panel for burn marks or older two-prong outlets, and confirm that gutters and soil slope water away from the building.\n\nProperty type, build year, and regional weather conditions change what you need to inspect. Condominiums do not require individual roof, basement, or private yard drainage checks, but they demand questions about homeowners association dues, reserve funds, planned special assessments, and hallway upkeep. Older properties require specific environmental questions. If a home was built before 1978, sellers must provide the federal disclosure on lead hazards per the [EPA real estate disclosure rule](https://www.epa.gov/lead/real-estate-disclosure). Homes from that era may also have old wiring, chipping paint, or lead solder, while homes built from 1978 to 1995 often used gray polybutylene plastic supply pipes. In our internal comparison, we saw these rules change the lists completely between a 1965 house in Denver and a modern Miami condo.\n\nComparing multiple properties works best when you record observations on the identical list during your visits. Use the checklist to mark each item as Good, Unsure, or Problem, and write the agent's exact responses in the \"Their answer\" boxes. After touring several properties, look at the summary cards to see how many unresolved questions and problems each house accumulated. The tool tallies your marks without assigning arbitrary scores, allowing you to review the specific flaws of each property side by side. Before touring, you can also check your overall budget with the [home affordability calculator](/mortgage/home-affordability-calculator/).\n\nA viewing gives you an essential initial impression, but it never substitutes for a professional inspection. A licensed home inspector checks the systems in detail once an offer is accepted. You can follow that detailed stage with our [home inspection checklist](/checklists/home-inspection-checklist/) and coordinate the broader purchase steps using the [home buying checklist](/checklists/home-buying-checklist/). What a seller is legally obligated to tell you is determined by state disclosure laws, and flood exposure should always be verified on the [FEMA flood map service center](https://msc.fema.gov/portal/home).\n\nBefore you head to an open house or scheduled walkthrough, print your customized checklist so you have a physical sheet in hand. Ask the agent the tough questions while you are standing together in the home, write down their explanations, and systematically inspect the plumbing, electrical fixtures, and structure yourself. Using the recorded problems across each property helps you decide which house deserves a formal offer and an inspection contingent contract.",
  "workedExample": "In our internal comparison, we ran the checklist's rules for different property profiles across our 33 item library to test how the questions and physical checks adjust. A buyer who tours a standard single-family house with the build year set to Not sure and freezing winters enabled receives 11 questions to ask the agent and 12 physical checks to perform.\n\nWhen we ran the rules for a home built in 1965 in Denver, Colorado, the checklist expanded to 12 questions and 14 physical checks. It added questions about obtaining homeowners insurance in wildfire areas and checks for pre-1978 wiring, peeling paint, exposed pipes in exterior walls, and combustible brush near siding. Conversely, running the rules for a condo built after 2000 in Miami, Florida reduced the checklist to 11 questions and 7 checks. It removed basement, roof, foundation, and lead paint checks, while adding HOA assessment questions and hallway maintenance reviews.",
  "commonMistakes": [
    "Focusing on decorative staging and paint colors instead of examining the age of heating systems, roof shingles, and the main electrical panel.",
    "Failing to ask why the owners are moving or ignoring past transactions that fell apart during the inspection phase.",
    "Relying on memory after touring three or four properties in one afternoon instead of writing down notes and marks on the spot.",
    "Skipping basic physical checks like running water taps, testing window latches, and smelling for dampness in basements or crawlspaces.",
    "Treating a casual open house viewing as a replacement for a formal inspection conducted by a licensed home inspector."
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What are some good questions to ask at an open house?",
      "answer": "Ask the hosting agent why the owners decided to sell and how many days the property has been on the market. Inquire if any past offers were withdrawn after an inspection and ask for the age of the roof, furnace, and water heater. You should also request recent utility costs, annual property taxes, and any applicable homeowners association fees."
    },
    {
      "question": "What are the biggest red flags to avoid when buying a house?",
      "answer": "Significant red flags include water stains on ceilings, musty smells in the basement, and isolated patches of fresh paint that might conceal active leaks. You should also watch for wide or stair-step cracks in foundation walls, sticking interior doors, sagging roof lines, and scorched electrical panels. Discovering unpermitted renovation work or an inability to obtain homeowners insurance also signals serious trouble."
    },
    {
      "question": "What not to do when touring a house?",
      "answer": "Avoid skipping direct questions about past insurance claims, roof leaks, and previous failed offers. Do not forget to turn on water taps, test window locks, and inspect under sinks, and never rely on memory alone when viewing several homes in a single weekend."
    },
    {
      "question": "What should I look for when viewing a house for the first time?",
      "answer": "Look for visible signs of structural and moisture issues, including wall cracks, sloping floors, damp smells, and water marks on ceilings. Test water pressure in the bathrooms, inspect the electrical breaker box, and open windows to verify that frames are not stuck or leaking air. Outside, examine the roof line from the street and check that the ground slopes away from the foundation."
    },
